<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nechtan mac Der Ilei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/732">732</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Nechtan son of Der-Ilei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nechtan mac Der Ilei was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> from about 706 to 724 and briefly again around 728–729. He brought the Pictish church into line with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, adopting the Roman date of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> after consulting Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceolfrith">Ceolfrith</a> of Wearmouth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a>, and in 717 expelled the clergy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> who refused it. He retired to a monastery in 724 and was drawn into the civil wars that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts">Óengus son of Fergus</a> to power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nechtan, also called Naiton son of Dargart, succeeded his brother Bridei IV in 706. It has been argued that he is the Nechtan son of Dargart named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> in 710, whose father would be Dargart mac Finguine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-comgaill">Cenél Comgaill</a> kindred of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, who died in 686; on that basis, and because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> notes that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> allowed matrilineal succession in exceptional cases, Der-Ilei is thought to have been his mother. His kin may have included Ciniod, killed in 713, and Talorcan son of Drestan, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholl</a>, whom Nechtan held captive in the same year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Around 710 Abbot Ceolfrid of Monkwearmouth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> wrote to Nechtan on the dating of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>, in a letter preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s Ecclesiastical History of the English People. It treats its reader as an educated man, which led the historian Thomas Owen Clancy to call Nechtan a philosopher king. Ceolfrid also sent masons and craftsmen to help build stone churches, and Bede's claim that Nechtan dedicated his kingdom to Saint Peter has linked him with the Peterkirks at Rosemarkie, Duffus and Restenneth. In 711 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> report a Pictish defeat at Northumbrian hands in Mag Manonn.</p><p>In 724, according to the Annals of Tigernach, Nechtan entered a monastery and left the throne to Drest, probably his half-brother's son. Drest had him imprisoned in 726, perhaps by moving him to a monastery controlled by his own partisans. By 728 Nechtan had returned to the struggle against Drest and Alpín, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts">Óengus son of Fergus</a> defeated Alpín a second time Nechtan was restored to the kingship; Óengus's army then beat Nechtan's enemies at Monith Carno, perhaps Cairn o' Mount.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nechtan died in 732. It has been suggested that the St Andrews Sarcophagus was made for his remains, though it is more generally thought to have been made for Óengus himself. His reforms are seen as having brought Pictland closer to Northumbria, with notable results in art, and his expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> clergy as marking the coming of age of an independent Pictish church that nonetheless remained close to Iona and to Ireland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nechtan mac Der-Ilei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 710 King <b>Nechtan son of Der-Ilei</b> wrote to Abbot Ceolfrith of Wearmouth and Jarrow asking for arguments on the dating of Easter and for masons to build a stone church, and in 717 he expelled the clergy of Iona who refused the Roman Easter.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The reported expulsion of Ionan monks and clergy by <b>Nechtan mac Der Ilei</b> in 717 may have been linked to the dispute over the dating of Easter and the tonsure, where Nechtan seems to have supported Roman usage, or may have been meant to increase royal power over…</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
